<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
      Light palette.

      Every brush the app uses is keyed here, and every consumer references it
      with DynamicResource so swapping this dictionary at runtime re-themes the
      live window. Nothing in the views may hard-code a colour.

      ── The elevation ladder ────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The three big surfaces are spread across four evenly-spaced steps of
      lightness rather than bunched at the top:

          SurfaceSunken   #E6E8EB   91%   wells — switch tracks, search, chips
          SurfaceAlt      #ECEEF1   93%   chrome — sidebar and top bar
          Background      #F2F4F7   95%   the canvas the cards sit on
          Surface         #FFFFFF  100%   cards — where the content is read

      (Percentages are mean channel: across a near-white range, what the eye
      reads as depth is lightness. The contrast ratios quoted below are the
      real relative-luminance ones.)

      An earlier version had SurfaceAlt, Surface and a #F7F8FB Background all
      between 98% and 100%. Two things went wrong at once: the window emitted
      near-maximum light over its whole area, which is fatiguing to sit in front
      of, and a 1.16:1 card border on white left the cards reading as one flat
      sheet instead of distinct panels. Reserving pure white for the cards is
      what makes the structure legible — the card edge is now 1.39:1, and the
      canvas it sits on is visibly a canvas.

      The ordering is deliberate and matches Dark.xaml: SurfaceAlt is a recessed
      variant of Surface, and SurfaceSunken is below both. Only the position of
      Background differs between the themes, which is the usual dark-mode
      inversion — in dark the canvas is the most recessed surface, in light it
      sits just under the cards.

      Pure white is fine — and correct — for the cards. It is the page-sized
      expanses of it that cause the glare.

      ── Why the neutrals are nearly neutral ─────────────────────────────────
      These were first written blue-tinted — #E8EDF5 and friends, an R↔B
      channel spread of 13. They are now spread 3-5, close to the near-neutral
      greys that mature light themes settle on (#F3F3F5 in Willow, #F5F4F0 in
      Flow — both measured off screenshots rather than eyeballed).

      That is not only taste. The accent is ALREADY blue (#2C6FD1), so a
      blue-cast canvas puts the accent in competition with its own background
      and blunts it. Against a neutral grey the blue reads unmistakably as the
      colour, which is what an accent is for. A whisper of cool is kept — these
      are not dead grey — because they sit under a blue accent, and a fully
      neutral field under a blue accent reads as unfinished.
    -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Background"  Color="#F2F4F7"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Surface"     Color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.SurfaceAlt"  Color="#ECEEF1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.SurfaceSunken" Color="#E6E8EB"/>
    <!-- Sits at 1.28:1 against the canvas and 1.39:1 against a card: a hairline
         that is actually visible, which the old #E3E7EF was not. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Border"      Color="#D8DBDF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.BorderSoft"  Color="#E9EBEE"/>
    <!-- 8% black rather than 6%: an overlay has to hold up on the darker canvas
         and sidebar as well as on a white card, and 6% disappeared on the two. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Hover"       Color="#14000000"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Selected"    Color="#1A2C6FD1"/>

    <!-- #1B2029, not near-black: 16.4:1 on a card and 14.8:1 on the canvas.
         Maximum contrast is not the same thing as comfortable contrast, and the
         letter edges are where that shows up. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Text"        Color="#1B2029"/>
    <!-- #58616F clears 4.5:1 on every surface it lands on (6.2:1 on a card,
         5.7:1 on the canvas, 5.3:1 on the sidebar, 5.0:1 on a switch track),
         where the old #6B7280 fell to 4.4:1 against the canvas. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.TextMuted"   Color="#58616F"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.TextInverse" Color="#FFFFFF"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Accent"      Color="#2C6FD1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.AccentHover" Color="#1F5FBF"/>
    <!-- Light enough to read as a *selected* tint against the sidebar at 84%
         (the nav row has no border to help it) while still sitting above the
         90% canvas for the filter pills. The old #E8F0FC was level with the new
         canvas and would have vanished into it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.AccentSoft"  Color="#ECF3FE"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Ok"          Color="#15803D"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.OkSoft"      Color="#E7F6EC"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Warn"        Color="#B45309"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.WarnSoft"    Color="#FDF3E2"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Danger"      Color="#B91C1C"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.DangerHover" Color="#991B1B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.DangerSoft"  Color="#FDECEC"/>

    <!-- Chart series — the four actions an alert can carry, in the order the
         donut and its legend render them: blocked, killed, kill_failed,
         detected_only. These are their own keys rather than reuses of
         Ok/Warn/Danger because a chart needs four hues that stay distinct from
         each other at a few pixels wide, not semantic colours that also have
         to stay legible as small text. Unchanged by the surface rework: they
         sit on cards, which are still white. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Chart1"      Color="#2C6FD1"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Chart2"      Color="#8B5CF6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Chart3"      Color="#F5B301"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Chart4"      Color="#A21CAF"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="App.Shadow"      Color="#33000000"/>

</ResourceDictionary>
